survey.method:
Read-only survey, done 2026-11-11. Our tree: tuned4 cd5482ece (worktree wt-tuned4). Scratch files: /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-Lichii/e57b64ae-d8d0-5bc0-9be5-b5fd2191474e/scratchpad/bt-survey/.

How I checked what we already carry: for each fork commit I matched its subject against `git log 17baed716..tuned4` and nx2, then grepped tuned4's source for the commit's identifiers (Fritchii renames commits when porting, so subjects alone miss some). Sources covered:
- the 84 upstream PR heads already fetched;
- 33 newer PR heads (#1241-#1311), fetched into refs/remotes/upstream-pr2/<n>;
- Fritchii oot-gather-fix 6649abc4f (v5.8.2);
- CheezyEmu 4baa89ec5 and Sharkiix v3.1.0.
Upstream nx2 is still 17baed716 and Eglantine nx2 is still 78743f30c (checked with ls-remote). kraibse's Eglantine commits b1e650849e, f27522bfb9 and d3612f82b1 are not on Eglantine nx2, so I read them through Fritchii's cherry-picks 85a33019b, d20c32704 and 1c8474ee9. The GitHub API and GraphQL were blocked in this session, so PR text comes from commit messages only.

What the r50 log (ReportFastDraws and the GPU_PROFILE lines, gameplay at about 47-50 fps) shows. This corrects one premise of the task:
- Delta draws are NOT zero: 29-42k delta draws a second against about 48k full draws a second. A full draw costs 5.7-6.3 us to prepare, a delta draw 1.0-1.2 us.
- What ends the fast-draw chain, per second ("by last break"):
  - register change 27k, by method: 0x700 (vertex_streams[0] stride/enable) 10.3k; 0x4da (blend) 3.8k; 0x460/0x464/0x466 (vertex_attrib_format) 3.7k; 0x853 (pipelines[5] program offset, a real change) 1.9k; 0x372 (polygon_offset_fill) 1.7k; 0x64f (viewport_clip_control) 1.5k;
  - deferred call (back macros, FrontRunOnBack) 12-14k;
  - delta overflow 2.8k;
  - handle data written 5.8-6.4k;
  - pipelines without delta draws (computed handles) 1.4-4.1k.
  So most full draws come from a handful of break causes that the open upstream PRs remove.
- Spin mutex contention on the back: 843/s, 69.8 ms/s (51.6 ms/s in the next window), about 1.0-1.4 ms a frame. The only SpinRecursiveMutex instances are the buffer cache's and the texture cache's mutexes (buffer_cache_base.h:555, texture_cache_base.h:494).
- Buffer downloads: 228-256 MB/s in 17-21k copies.
- Fence: 12-14k fences a second (34-38 ms/s) plus 8-9k skipped fences (13-17 ms/s); 2,100-2,200 submits a second, and only 5-30 a second are empty.
- 3D register writes: 3.7M a second, 1.22-1.28M of them same-value. The method zone runs 5.0-5.3M methods a second at about 32 ns each.
- Compute: 7.9-9.1k dispatches a second take 120-126 ms/s, about 14 us per dispatch.

Estimates: converting about 35k full draws a second to delta draws saves at most about 160 ms/s, roughly 3 ms a frame. Each break removed can expose the next break, so I expect 1.5-2.5 ms. That makes the fast-draw items below the biggest single lever, and they all come from upstream Lichii.

Items already in tuned4, not repeated in the list:
- #974 (cf811c16a, 974469aa5, 9389e4f68);
- #1059 (81ee8c090);
- #1088 AMD 512-draw submissions (vk_rasterizer.cpp:4335-4340);
- #1094, #1244, #933, #954, #1095, #1245 (in nx2), #1246 (7fcfb6412);
- #805's split, back macros and fenced-release commits;
- Fritchii EXP9/18/29/35/36/42/43.
Gains quoted below are the authors' own unless I say otherwise; I measured nothing.

ideas.mechanism:
In tuned4's GpuSplit, the front writes write_position (gpu_split.h:753) in every Commit (gpu_split.h:669-670) and reads it in every Reserve (658). It sits on the same 64-byte line as the members the back reads for every entry: `ring` (750, read at gpu_split.cpp:871 `&ring[index]`), `system` (`*system` in every Execute call), `starts` (EntryTag) and `front_macros`. The layout probe (clang, libstdc++; these leading members are the same types on MSVC) gives:
- system at offset 8, ring 32, write_position 40, last_writer_position 48, all on line 0;
- the front's published_local/publish_every/front_engine on line 5125;
- back_engine/back_entry_start on line 5126. The back stores back_entry_start for every entry (gpu_split.cpp:869).

Execute and CallMethod are opaque calls, so the back reloads ring and system for each entry. After each front Commit, that load misses and fetches the line from the front's core. #1251 puts alignas(128) on write_position and on back_engine. That moves the front's hot positions off line 0 and gives the back's per-entry fields their own 128-byte pair. It is layout only.

gpu_split.cpp:741-743 and 765-767 call Nx2FastDraw::Break(OtherEngine) for every Kind::Method and Kind::Multi entry of an engine without reports_fast_draw_breaks (only Maxwell3D sets it, maxwell_3d.cpp:67). Each call is g_serial.fetch_add, a locked RMW that drains the store buffer, plus a store (nx2_fast_draw.h:89-94). That includes sunk state writes and compute plain stores (kepler_compute.cpp:513-519), and neither can change what a 3D draw resolves to.

The proposed rule:
- break in Kind::Engine (gpu_split.cpp:685-687) when the old or the new engine doesn't report breaks;
- keep the per-entry break only for executable methods (engine->execution_mask[method]) and for Direct writes the engine doesn't flag as plain stores;
- skip it for sunk entries and compute native_plain_methods.

Breaking on both switch directions also covers a deferred 3D draw that ConsumeSink issues while compute is selected. Keeping breaks on executable entries covers a FastDrawNeutral Defer that draws between two compute launches with no engine switch.

Compute macros replay the QMD and grid fields for every dispatch. FrontRunMacro's emit (kepler_compute.cpp:2041-2052) and FrontCallMethod (1795-1806) send each as a MacroWrite or Method entry. On the back the entry pays:
- Execute's non-fast path: the Break atomic at gpu_split.cpp:741 and, under profiling, a full Scope at 745;
- ConsumeSink;
- the virtual KeplerCompute::CallMethod, whose native_plain_methods path only stores the value into native_registers and regs (kepler_compute.cpp:513-519).

Fritchii keeps a plain_sent bitset on the front. A write to a native_plain_methods register is dropped when the front already sent that register and its copy holds the value, because then both back copies hold it.

In tuned4, every other path that changes those front copies must reset plain_sent for the register. Those paths are ListCallContext::SetRegister (kepler_compute.cpp:1640-1649), the QMD takeover (GetFrontQmd().MaybeTakeOver) and FrontApply cases. This is the stale-0xb0 class of bug Fritchii fixed in 5c173ca274.

Port Fritchii 753c422e13 ('nx2 profile: compute dispatch zones, read flush zone and compute binding counters', profile-only). It adds:
- zones 'compute lookup' (CurrentComputePipeline), 'compute pointers' (the BindComputeStorageBuffer loop, vk_compute_pipeline.cpp:455-463), 'compute views' (SynchronizeDescriptors to FillImageViews, 465-551), 'compute resolve' (UpdateComputeBuffers) and 'compute synchronize' (BindHostComputeBuffers, 693-697);
- a per-second 'compute bindings' line: storage bytes bound, bytes SynchronizeBuffer uploaded, VirtualRangeCache::Query hits and misses, StorageBufferBinding cache hits and misses;
- the top 8 compute programs by dispatch count.

Add three things of our own:
- a 'compute record' FineScope around the Record calls, NoteWorkRecorded and SubmitNx2ListCopies (vk_rasterizer.cpp:2357-2411), plus a 'compute FSR' scope around TryOcarinaDlss(replace) at 2370-2374;
- counters for the pending_download_ranges length seen by MarkWrittenBuffer (buffer_cache.h:3604), for the SubmitNx2ListCopies flushes that follow a dispatch (vk_rasterizer.cpp:2473-2474), and for the MasterSemaphore::Refresh calls made from NoteWorkRecorded(true) (buffer_cache.h:950-952);
- a 'Slow dispatch N us' line for dispatches over 200 us. Model it on 'Slow clear' (vk_rasterizer.cpp:1971/1985): shader hash, the sub-zone times, buffers created or joined, and bytes uploaded.

Graphics already skips most of the resolve for delta draws (Nx2FastDraw; full 5.5-6.3 us against delta 1.0-1.2 us). Compute re-resolves everything on every dispatch:
- the CurrentComputePipeline lookup: GpuToCpuAddress, TryGet, VerifyTrapGuards, BypassOotNetwork, ProcessGameDlss, compute_cache.try_emplace (vk_pipeline_cache.cpp:1223-1283, shader_cache.cpp:294-349);
- per SSBO, StorageBufferBinding plus a write-extent Read<u64> (buffer_cache.h:1965-2056);
- handle reads and GetSamplerId per texture (vk_compute_pipeline.cpp:476-546);
- FillImageViews, then FindBuffer per binding (3554-3589) and PushImageDescriptors.

Memo per pipeline. Key: the QMD fields that matter (CB enable mask, CB addresses and sizes, linked_tsc, block dims and shared size), the TIC/TSC table addresses and limits plus texture_bindings_serial, MapSerial, a buffer-cache epoch (buffer creations and deletions) and a texture-cache epoch (view and sampler deletions). It also includes a copy of the CB words that handles and SSBO pointers are read from (desc.cbuf_index/cbuf_offset, a handful of words). On a hit, reuse the pipeline pointer, the views and samplers, the SSBO Bindings and the image-descriptor payload. Still run every dispatch:
- FlushWork, TickWork;
- UBO streaming (the CB contents differ per object);
- SynchronizeBuffer and MarkWrittenBuffer;
- MarkModification of written images;
- the descriptor push and the Record calls.

Pipelines with handle programs (desc.handle_program != ~0U) and byte-addressed shadows stay excluded, as graphics' ComputedHandles blocker does. Add EDEN_NX2_DISPATCH_MEMO_VERIFY to run the full path and compare.

ideas.correctness_risks:
A key that misses a dependency binds stale resources:
- a TIC/TSC entry rewritten in place (covered only if texture_bindings_serial bumps on table content changes; SynchronizeDescriptors does, texture_cache.h:1403-1415);
- a pointer word in a CB that the CPU or GPU rewrote (covered by comparing the words);
- a buffer or view deleted and its id reused (the epochs).

The FSR 4 replacement pass and Nx2ListIndirect slot dispatches should bypass the memo, which keeps CheezyEmu's FSR path and the list path untouched. Verify mode: FrameDump of Kokiri water, grass and Castle Town against a memo-off run.

ideas.expected_saving_ms:
0.5-1.0 ms/frame. Candidates (r50 at 426 s):
- attribute formats: 3,998+762+548+379 ≈ 5.7k/s ≈ 120 draws/frame;
- overflow: 2,335/s ≈ 50 draws/frame;
- handle data written: 5,669/s ≈ 120 draws/frame, false positives unknown.
That is ~290 candidates/frame. With 40-75% converting: 115-220 draws x 4.55 us = 0.5-1.0 ms.
